Features of StreamsJava Streams provide a flexible way to process sequences of data using functional-style operations. They simplify data transformations without modifying the original data source.Declarative Processing: Focuses on what operation to perform rather than writing explicit iteration logic.Lazy Evaluation: Delays intermediate operations until a terminal operation starts processing.Operation Chaining: Combines multiple operations into a single processing pipeline.Parallel Execution: Can divide suitable workloads across multiple threads using parallel streams.Non-Storage: Processes elements from a source without acting as a data structure itself.Functional Style: Works naturally with lambda expressions and method references.Stream PipelineA Stream Pipeline is a sequence of operations performed on a stream to process data efficiently. It consists of a data source, one or more intermediate operations, and a terminal operation that produces the final result.Intermediate operations (such as filter(), map(), and sorted()) are lazy and execute only when a terminal operation is invoked.A pipeline processes elements in a streamlined manner, allowing multiple operations to be combined into a single flow. How Stream Pipeline Works1. SourceThe source provides data to the stream. It can be a collection, array, file, or another supported data source.Example:List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30, 40);
Stream<Integer> stream = numbers.stream();2. Intermediate OperationsIntermediate operations transform a stream into another stream. They are lazy, meaning processing generally begins only when a terminal operation is invoked.Common intermediate operations include:filter() –> Selects elements that satisfy a condition.map() –> Transforms elements into another form.sorted() –> Sorts stream elements.distinct() – Removes duplicate elements.skip() –> Skips a specified number of elements.Example:
            Java

    import java.util.*;

public class StreamIntermediate {
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        List<Integer> numbers
            = Arrays.asList(5, 10, 20, 10, 30, 40);

        numbers.stream()
            .filter(n -> n > 10)
            .map(n -> n * 2)
            .distinct()
            .sorted()
            .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}

